   "source": [
    "### Introduction\n",
    "The global preferences survey measures key measures core economic and psychological traits across tens of thousands of people in dozens of countries. The hypotheses analyzed through this data report cover multiple facets of how different conditions impact trust globally. Hypothesis 1 by Amelia Fischer is countries where more languages are spoken will have lower trust scores. This due to the assumption that multilingual countries breed language barriers, make it difficult to intrepret and trust information. Hypothesis 2 by Vaibhava Rajesh is that higher trust across countries is correlated with higher risk taking. The assumption is that individuals with higher trust may be more inclined to engage in risk taking. Hypothesis 3 by Bonnie is that People with higher trust scores will have higher altruism scores than people with lower trust scores. This results in the following research question: How do structural factors, such as linguistic diversity, and individual traits, such as risk taking and altruism, relate to variations in trust across countries?"
   ]
  },
  {
