   "source": [
    "### Hypothesis #1: Countries where more languages are spoken will have lower trust scores.\n",
    "#### Amelia Fischer \n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Single-language countries: n=49, mean trust=0.039\n",
      "Multi-language countries:  n=27,  mean trust=-0.105\n",
      "TtestResult(statistic=np.float64(2.158917389226313), pvalue=np.float64(0.03409878391869565), df=np.float64(74.0))\n"
     ]
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 600x600 with 3 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "from scipy import stats\n",
    "country_df = gl_df.groupby('country').agg(\n",
    "    n_languages=('language', 'nunique'),\n",
    "    mean_trust=('trust', 'mean')\n",
    ").reset_index()\n",
    "\n",
    "single = country_df[country_df['n_languages'] == 1]['mean_trust']\n",
    "multi  = country_df[country_df['n_languages'] > 1]['mean_trust']\n",
    "print(f\"Single-language countries: n={len(single)}, mean trust={single.mean():.3f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Multi-language countries:  n={len(multi)},  mean trust={multi.mean():.3f}\")\n",
    "print(stats.ttest_ind(single, multi))\n",
    "\n",
    "g = sns.jointplot(data=country_df, x='n_languages', y='mean_trust', color='blueviolet', kind='reg')\n",
    "g.set_axis_labels('Number of Languages Spoken', 'Mean Trust Score')\n",
    "g.figure.suptitle('Language Diversity vs. Trust Score', y=1.02)\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The hypothesis tested whether countries with greater language diversity would have lower average trust scores. An independent samples t-test compared mean trust scores between single language countries (n=49) and multi-language countries (n=27). Single language countries had a mean trust score of 0.039, while multi-language countries had a mean of -0.105. The t-test produced a statistic of 2.16 and a p value of 0.034, which is below the 0.05 threshold, meaning the difference is statistically significant. The jointplot visualization supports this finding, showing a downward trend. As the number of languages spoken increases, the average trust score decreases. This suggests that linguistic diversity is associated with lower social trust, possibly because multilingual societies tend to have greater cultural diversity, which could be linked to reduced generalized trust. Overall, the analysis supports the hypothesis: countries where more languages are spoken tend to have lower average trust scores."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Hypothesis #2: Higher trust across countries is correlated with higher risk taking.\n",
    "#### Vaibhava Sri Rajesh Khanna"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 15,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "('Correlation Between Risk taking and Trust', np.float64(0.035705953080578695))\n"
     ]
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 640x480 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "Correlation = (\"Correlation Between Risk taking and Trust\", gl_df['risktaking'].corr(gl_df[\"trust\"]))\n",
    "print(Correlation)\n",
    "\n",
    "import seaborn as sns\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "\n",
    "sns.boxplot(x=\"trust\", y=\"risktaking\", data=gl_df, palette=\"Blues\",hue=\"trust\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"Trust Level\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Risk taking\")\n",
    "plt.title(\"H2: Trust vs Risk taking\")\n",
    "plt.xticks(rotation=45, ha='right')\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The boxplot shows how risk taking varies across different levels of trust. Each box represents the distribution of risk taking scores for a given trust level, as well as the median and overall spread. While there is a slight increase in median risk taking at higher levels of trust, the distributions overlap heavily. This means that there are little differences. This is further supported by the correlation result (r = 0.036), indicating a very weak positive relationship between trust and risk taking. In conclusion, changes in trust are not strongly associated with changes in risk taking behavior. The wide spread of values within each trust level proves risk taking varies a lot, regardless of trust levels. The visualization and correlation prove that there is no clear or consistent relationship between trust and risk taking. Therefore, my hypothesis is not supported, higher trust levls do not correlate to higher risk taking. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Hypothesis #3: \n",
    "#### Bonnie Yuan\n",
    "Hypothesis #3: There is a positive relationship between trust and altruism, such that higher trust scores are associated with higher altruism scores."
   ]
