   ],
   "source": [
    "df = pd.read_csv(\"finalprojectdatasets/Global Preferences Survey/gps_data.csv\")\n",
    "df = df[df[\"trust\"] == df[\"trust\"]]\n",
    "df = df[df[\"altruism\"] == df[\"altruism\"]]\n",
    "\n",
    "Alt_Correlation= gl_df[\"trust\"].corr(gl_df[\"altruism\"])\n",
    "print (Alt_Correlation)\n",
    "trust_median = df[\"trust\"].median()\n",
    "high_trust = df[df[\"trust\"] > trust_median]\n",
    "low_trust = df[df[\"trust\"] <= trust_median]\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.boxplot([high_trust[\"altruism\"], low_trust[\"altruism\"]], tick_labels=[\"High Trust\", \"Low Trust\"])\n",
    "plt.title(\"Altruism by Trust Group\")\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"Trust Group\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Altruism\")\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Median trust:\")\n",
    "print(\"Mean altruism for high trust group:\", high_trust[\"altruism\"].mean())\n",
    "print(\"Mean altruism for low trust group:\", low_trust[\"altruism\"].mean())"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "My hypothesis was that people with higher trust scores would also have higher altruism scores. To test this, I divided the respondents into a high trust group and a low trust group based on the median trust score. Then I used a boxplot to compare altruism in the two groups. The median trust score was about -0.144. The mean altruism score for the high trust group was about 0.144, while the mean altruism score for the low trust group was about -0.122. This means the high trust group had a higher average altruism score. In the boxplot, the center of the high trust group is also slightly higher than the low trust group. The two groups still overlap a lot, so the difference is not very large. The overall pattern still supports my hypothesis, suggesting trust and altruism are positively related. People who trust others more may also be more willing to act in a generous or helpful way."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Conclusion\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "jp-MarkdownHeadingCollapsed": true
   },
   "source": [
    "This report examined how different factors relate to trust across countries using data from the Global Preferences Survey. The research question explored how structural factors, such as linguistic diversity, and individual traits, like risk taking and altruism, are associated with trust. Three hypotheses were tested: that countries with more languages would have lower trust, that higher trust would be associated with greater risk taking, and that higher trust would be associated with higher altruism. The results partially supported the research question and hypotheses. The difference in trust between single language and multilingual countries was statistically significant (p = 0.034). Multilingual countries show slightly lower average trust scores, supporting the first hypothesis. This suggested that linguistic diversity may create barriers to communication, lessening overall trust. However, hypothesis two indicated that the relationship between trust and risk taking was not supported, due to the a low association(r = 0.036). This implies that a person's willingness to take risk is independent of how much trust they have for others. The third hypothesis supported the correlation between trust and altruism, showing a weak positive correlation (r = 0.168), showing that individuals with higher trust tend to exhibit slightly higher levels of altruism. In conclusion, the takeway is that trust is more closely related to behaviors like altruism, rather than to individual risk taking tendencies. While factors like linguistic diversity may have some influence on trust, behavioral traits have more of an impact. Trust does not necessarily serve as a predictor of behavior. Future research on trust could extend on these findings by separating individual and country level effects to identify how structural factors contribute to levels of trust."
   ]
  }
